180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 99 0 R
/E (Pastoral mobility has always been a livelihood strategy since time immemorial, not a crisis in itself. But across the East and Horn of Africa, key informant interviews for this study show that climate stress is changing the character of that mobility by stretching migration routes, forcing settlement in unfamiliar or contested territory, and pushing growing numbers of households into displacement rather than planned seasonal movement. Who experiences this shift, and how and why is not uniform. Mobility decisions are shaped by gender, age, disability, marital status and household position, producing sharply different risks for those who move, those who stay behind, and those who are displaced involuntarily.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 99 0 R
/E (This brief applies a gender equality and social inclusion \(GESI\) lens to pastoral mobility, arguing that migration, displacement and protection cannot be addressed as a single undifferentiated policy problem. Women who move face acute protection risks; women left behind absorb the full burden of household survival with reduced support; and women who are also disabled, widowed, elderly or from minority clans face compounding exclusion at every stage. Hence, policy responses that do not disaggregate by who moves, who stays and who is displaced will continue to miss the specific protection failures this evidence identifies.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
