220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (The findings on pastoral mobility align closely with recent academic and institutional analysis of climate-linked mobility in the Horn of Africa. Etzold's \(2023\) reflections on intersecting \(im\)mobilities in Kenya, drawing on fieldwork in Marsabit County, identify four distinct and simultaneously occurring forms of movement shaped by drought and conflict, notably pastoral mobility, sedentarization, forced displacement, and voluntary labour or educational mobility. This framework offers useful conceptual precision for this brief's core GESI argument, that "mobility" in pastoral contexts is not one phenomenon but several, each carrying a different risk profile for women depending on which form they experience.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Regional analysis from the Research and Evidence Facility on Migration in the Horn of Africa similarly finds that pastoral mobility, historically a voluntary and adaptive response to environmental variability, is increasingly characterized by longer forced movement and rising numbers of cross-border migrants and internally displaced persons, even as many national and regional policy responses continue to frame mobility primarily as a problem to be managed through sedentarization rather than a livelihood strategy to be protected \(Research and Evidence Facility, 2022\). This mirrors the finding that formal systems have not kept pace with the changing character of pastoral movement.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (The gender-based violence risks facing migrating and displaced women identified in this study are substantiated by a growing body of epidemiological evidence. A systematic review and meta-analysis of GBV among refugees and internally displaced women across Africa found that displaced women face elevated risk of violence both during flight and after arrival in camps, where protection mechanisms are frequently insufficient, with economic dependence on male companions or humanitarian aid identified as a significant contributing factor to ongoing vulnerability \(Tadesse et al, 2024\). A cross-sectional study of internally displaced women in Northwest Ethiopia found a one-year GBV prevalence of 37.9 percent, with young women aged 18 to 29 and those lacking social protection at significantly elevated risk \(Workie at al., 2023\). )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
