268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Widows, divorced women, adolescent girls, elderly women, women with disabilities, displaced women and minority clan women experience overlapping barriers that are not simply additive but compounding, as each additional axis of exclusion narrows access to assets, services, mobility and protection further. Disability-focused respondents specifically highlighted that infrastructure, communication and programme design frequently fail to accommodate women with disabilities during both migration and displacement.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Again, this is strongly corroborated by recent academic research focused specifically on women with disabilities in Eastern Africa's climate context, which documents significant barriers to evacuation, early-warning access and policy participation, alongside a near-total absence of disability specific provisions in national climate adaptation policy frameworks \(Nyasimi et al, 2026\). The same body of literature notes that data on displaced persons with disabilities is rarely disaggregated by gender, making it difficult for humanitarian actors to even identify the scale of the compounded risk this population faces \(UN-DESA, 2016\). )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
