/Title (Mobility in pastoral systems has historically functioned as an adaptive strategy, as a way of following rainfall, pasture and water rather than a symptom of crisis. Recent academic work on the Horn of Africa describes this more precisely as a set of intersecting \(im\)mobilities: traditional pastoral movement, sedentarization, forced displacement, and voluntary labour or educational migration all occurring simultaneously within the same communities and often within the same households, shaped in different ways by drought and conflict.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1269 0 R
/Next 1274 0 R
>>
endobj
1274 0 obj
<<
/Title (This brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study through key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa. That evidence shows these different forms of mobility are not experienced equally. Respondents distinguished consistently between women and girls who migrate and who face acute exposure to GBV, exploitation and unsafe travel and women who are left behind, who absorb full household responsibility with fewer resources and reduced social support. Both groups face risks; the risks are simply different, and policy responses designed around one will not adequately protect the other as their gender alone does not make their experiences homogenous.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1269 0 R
/Prev 1273 0 R
>>
endobj
